  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Simplify before differentiating",
      "body": "<p>The denominator is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Expand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and divide each term by this power.</p>",
      "recap": "A radical quotient may become a short sum of powers.",
      "pitfall": "Dividing a sum means dividing every term.",
      "pause": "Write y as three powers of x.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "Simplify to powers before choosing a differentiation rule."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Apply the power rule term by term",
      "body": "<p>The simplified expression is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> on each term.</p>",
      "recap": "Multiply by the old exponent, then subtract one from it.",
      "pitfall": "The negative coefficient and negative exponent in the middle term give a positive derivative term.",
      "pause": "Differentiate before substituting x=2.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "The power rule works for the fractional and negative powers used here."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Combine the exact values",
      "body": "<p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, express all three derivative terms with denominator <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. For example, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Common radical denominators keep the arithmetic exact.",
      "pitfall": "The tangent gradient is the derivative value, not the original y-value.",
      "pause": "Add the three derivative contributions and simplify.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l05",
          "reminder": "Use index laws to simplify fractional powers at a positive input."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the gradient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer B.</strong></p>",
